## Forbidden Patterns

### ❌ AVOID These Anti-Patterns
```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Direct fetch usage without error handling
const fetchData = async () => {
  const response = await fetch('/api/data');
  const data = await response.json();
  return data;
};

// ❌ WRONG - No request/response typing
const apiCall = async (url: string, data: any): Promise<any> => {
  return axios.post(url, data);
};

// ❌ WRONG - Hardcoded URLs
const getUser = async (id: string) => {
  return axios.get(`http://localhost:3000/api/users/${id}`);
};

// ❌ WRONG - No authentication handling
const protectedCall = async () => {
  return axios.get('/api/protected');
};

// ❌ WRONG - No retry logic for failed requests
const unreliableCall = async () => {
  try {
    return await axios.get('/api/unreliable');
  } catch (error) {
    throw error; // No retry attempt
  }
};

// ❌ WRONG - Mixing business logic with API calls
const getUserAndUpdateUI = async (id: string) => {
  const user = await axios.get(`/api/users/${id}`);
  document.getElementById('username').textContent = user.data.name;
  return user.data;
};

// ❌ WRONG - No request cancellation
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData().then(setData);
}, [dependency]); // No cleanup

// ❌ WRONG - Poor error messages
const handleError = (error: any) => {
  alert('Error!'); // Generic error message
};
```

## API Integration Checklist

### Before Implementation
- [ ] API endpoints and contracts are documented
- [ ] Authentication strategy is defined
- [ ] Error handling strategy is planned
- [ ] Request/response types are defined

### During Development
- [ ] HTTP client is properly configured with interceptors
- [ ] All API calls are properly typed with TypeScript
- [ ] Authentication tokens are handled automatically
- [ ] Retry logic is implemented for appropriate errors
- [ ] Loading states are managed consistently

### Before Code Review
- [ ] Error handling provides user-friendly messages
- [ ] API responses are validated and typed
- [ ] Request cancellation is implemented where needed
- [ ] API calls are tested with mock data
- [ ] Performance is optimized with caching and deduplication

### Security Checklist
- [ ] Sensitive data is not logged
- [ ] Authentication tokens are stored securely
- [ ] API endpoints use HTTPS in production
- [ ] Request/response data is validated
- [ ] Rate limiting is handled gracefully

### Testing Checklist
- [ ] API services have unit tests
- [ ] Mock services are used for testing
- [ ] Error scenarios are tested
- [ ] Network delays are simulated in tests
- [ ] Integration tests cover critical API flows

**Remember: API integration is the backbone of modern web applications. Ensure robust error handling, type safety, and comprehensive testing to create reliable and maintainable API layers.**
